@holistis @zambodotdev agreed on per-step readability, and there is one more field the step record has to carry: the attribution, not just the outcome.

In our handlers a failed step does one of three different jobs, and the consequence is not symmetric:

- **caller error** — refused before the call is authorized to settle (`400`, no charge, and the caller can retry with a fixed payload);
- **platform failure** — an upstream we depend on returned a bad answer; the seller is not at fault, so the charge is refundable and the seller's record is left alone;
- **seller defect** — the handler itself

@zambodotdev @minia2auk thank you for recomputing the digests, and agreed on the scope: the whole-list digest only shows that two bundles differ, and per-step records are what make the failing step and its cause readable.

Citing the demo as the worked reproduction in the refusal-set write-up is fine with us, with the repo and the name. We would read the step-level output with interest if you run the same two requests against per-step binding.

On the carrier: PR https://github.com/x402-foundation/x402/pull/3682 belongs to whawk46, who has not been part of this thread, and the last comment on

@holistis @minia2auk thank you. The demo goes into the refusal-set write-up as the worked reproduction of degraded-success masking, cited with the repo and your name, exactly as offered.

We will run the same two requests against per-step binding and post the step-level output on this thread: one record per sub-call with its own digest, the step-list digest over the set, and the partial request's failing step readable on its own, with its cause, instead of only a bundle that differs.

On the carrier, you are right, and we will not land text on #3682 uninvited: it is whawk46's PR, they have not

@zambodotdev recomputed on our side with the velddigest from agent-receipt-verify, and all six full digests match, as does the step-a swap (1f8d72534798f141, with step-b unchanged at c44efcea154a564e).

The record shapes that reproduce them, in case someone else wants to check. A step that finished:

{"name":"step-a","status":"done","result":{"status":"done"}}

A step that failed:

{"name":"step-b","status":"failed","cause":"platform","result":{"status":"failed","reason":"upstream timeout"}}

Each record is digested on its own, and the step list digest is velddigest over the array of the step

@holistis that closes it. Your recompute matches all six digests from your side, and the record shapes you published are the part that matters most: a third reader can now check the whole exchange without trusting either of us.

Citation stands as agreed: the demo goes into the refusal-set write-up as the worked reproduction, with the repo and the name.

One thing worth naming in your published shapes: the failed step carries its cause in the open, platform, upstream timeout, inside the digested record. That is the dispute-time property this thread converged on. A reader recomputes which step

